<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tunisia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tunisian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tunisia is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, corresponding largely to the ancient region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>, later the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, whose empire stretched from the Atlantic to Tunisia. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was the scene of the final <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> defeat in North Africa, which accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> place in its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tunisia.jpg" alt="Tunisia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Various Wikimedia Commons' contributors · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tunisia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tunisia lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> to the west and south-west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> to the south-east. It covers 163,610 square kilometres and has a coastline of about 1,300 kilometres. The Dorsal, the eastern extension of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a>, crosses the country north-eastward; north of it lies the Tell, a region of low hills and plains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, a coastal plain on the east, is among the world's premier areas of olive cultivation, and much of the south is semi-arid and desert. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, the capital, stands on a hill slope above the lake of Tunis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tunisia was inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes at the beginning of recorded history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> began settling the coast from as early as the 12th century BCE, at Bizerte and Utica. Carthage, founded in the 9th century BCE, became the dominant power in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> after wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. According to the Greek writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timaeus-of-tauromenium">Timaeus of Tauromenium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> founded it in 814 BCE, though this is regarded as legend. Its people worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanit">Tanit</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Carthage was a client state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> until it was conquered in 146 BCE and made the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>Arab Muslims conquered Tunisia in the 7th century CE, and the migration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-sulaym">Banu Sulaym</a> tribes in the 11th and 12th centuries accelerated the process, and by about the 15th century the region was almost completely Arabised. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> took control in 1546 and held it until the French conquest of 1881. Tunisia became independent as the Tunisian Republic in 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Tunisian Revolution of 2011 overthrew President Zine El Abidine Ben Ali after 24 years in power and set off the wider Arab Spring. A new constitution was adopted on 26 January 2014, and a constitutional referendum in 2022 made it a unitary presidential republic.</p><p>The population is 12.1 million, the vast majority Arab and Muslim. Arabic is the official language, and French serves as an administrative and educational language without official status. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> is the first trading partner: Tunisia signed an Association Agreement with it in July 1995, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> country to do so.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tunisia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Roman Republic destroyed Carthage at the end of the Third Punic War in 146 BCE, it turned the city's remaining territory in what is now northern <b>Tunisia</b> into the province of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ibrahim's charge covered what is now eastern Algeria, <b>Tunisia</b> and Tripolitania, and in theory the whole Maghreb west of Cyrenaica, since the territory granted him was never demarcated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Over a reign of more than thirty years he steadily overwhelmed the Almoravids, captured their capital Marrakesh in 1147, assumed the title of caliph, the first Berber ruler to do so, and completed the conquest of the Maghreb from Morocco to <b>Tunisia</b> by 1160.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Napoleon III, colonial campaigns added Cochinchina, New Caledonia, and an expanded Senegal under Louis Faidherbe, and the Third Republic later pushed into <b>Tunisia</b>, Tonkin, Madagascar, and the Sahel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1207, after the Almohad caliph Muhammad al-Nasir had driven the Banu Ghaniya, Almoravid holdouts who had overrun much of the eastern Maghreb, out of Tunis, he appointed Abu Hafs’s son Abu Muhammad Abd al-Wahid governor of Ifriqiya, roughly modern <b>Tunisia</b> and eastern Algeria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…market town; Idris II developed it, moved his government there in 809 and founded the settlement of al-'Aliya, and welcomed two waves of Arab immigration, one from Córdoba in 818 and another from Aghlabid <b>Tunisia</b> in 824, which gave Fez a more Arab character than other Maghrebi cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
